+# Narrowed Veterinarian Reads Bypass the Vet Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The veterinarian repository's reads share one cache with no size bound and no eviction. Each distinct argument set becomes one lasting entry. `REQ-VET-003` adds reads narrowed by a specialty name that the caller types into the address. Any text is accepted, and a name no veterinarian holds is a normal request with an empty result.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache narrowed reads like the unnarrowed ones.** One annotation per read. Every distinct name a caller types adds an entry that never leaves, so any caller can grow the cache without limit.
+2. **Resolve the name to a stored specialty first, and cache by its identity.** The key space is bounded by the specialty list. It adds a second query and a lookup path for a data set measured in single digits.
+3. **Leave narrowed reads uncached** (chosen). Each narrowed request queries the database.
+
+## Decision
+
+Narrowed reads are not cached. The vet cache holds the unnarrowed reads only. Request-derived text never becomes a key in that cache.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Cache memory stays independent of caller input on the narrowing path.
+- Every narrowed request costs one database query plus its specialty loads. At the recorded size this is negligible.
+- Option 2 stays available if the directory grows and narrowed reads become hot, because it keeps the key space bounded.
+- The unnarrowed paged read is still keyed by the caller-chosen page number. That exposure predates this decision and is recorded in the threat model.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [Scale and Load](../system-design.md#scale-and-load)
+- [Persistence](../system-design.md#persistence)
+- [Threat Model](../system-design.md#threat-model)
